( Double-precision floats: an IEEE-754 double in two cells on the data
  stack, the low cell below the high cell, so 2dup 2swap 2drop 2over work on
  them. Literals use a d exponent in place of e: 1.5d0 6.02d23 -2.25d-3 1d6.
  Use df. to print. On chips without a double-precision FPU these run in
  software, about 1.5 to 2 times slower than single floats on an ESP32-C3.
  Primitives: df+ df- df* df/ df< df= s>df df>s d>df df>d f>df df>f dfsqrt
  dffloor dfceil dfround dftrunc. df>s and df>d truncate toward zero and
  saturate; NaN converts to 0. )

: df. s" %.15lg " fmt ;
: df> 2swap df< ;
: df<= 2over 2over df< >r df= r> | ;
: df>= 2swap df<= ;
: df0< 0 0 df< ;
: df0= 0 0 df= ;
: dfnegate 0x80000000 ^ ;
: dfabs 0x7fffffff & ;
: dfmin 2over 2over df> if 2swap fi 2drop ;
: dfmax 2over 2over df< if 2swap fi 2drop ;

( Forth-2012 names. df! stores the double low cell first, which is the
  native byte order on little-endian chips such as the ESP32 family, so C
  code can read it as a double. dfvariable is zero-initialized and stays
  writable in a ROM image. )
: df! dup >r cell + !c r> !c ;
: df@ dup @c swap cell + @c ;
: dfconstant >r >r : r> postpone literal r> postpone literal postpone ; ;
: dfvariable data-here 2 cells data-allot 0 0 2 pick df! constant ;
